<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shona are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwean plateau</a> and neighbouring areas. They formed the population of states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a>. Accounts of the Rozvi Empire describe the predominantly Shona-speaking population as a source of agricultural tribute.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 11th century the Zhizo moved west as newcomers of the Leopard’s Kopje tradition, probably speakers of an early form of <b>Shona</b>, arrived and built a large settlement at the site known as K2 or Bambandyanalo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mapungubwe">Kingdom of Mapungubwe · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Farming communities whose pottery archaeologists call Gumanye, regarded as ancestors of the Karanga branch of the <b>Shona</b>, lived at the site from around 1000, and from about the 12th century a ruling group began to enclose its granite hill with dry-stone walls: the radiocarbon dates from the site…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Contact with Swahili merchants, some of whom resided at the royal court, brought foreign goods and ideas into local society, although Islam made little headway among the <b>Shona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Regional government was delegated to subordinate chiefs drawn both from the Rozvi ruling lineage and from incorporated <b>Shona</b> dynasties, whose loyalty was maintained through cattle patronage and reinforced by the Changamire's symbolic connection to the Mwari cult at the Matopos shrines.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Zimbabwe adopted a <b>Shona</b> and Ndebele version, &quot;Ishe Komborera Africa&quot;, at independence in 1980 and replaced it in 1994; Namibia used the hymn at independence in March 1990 and replaced it in 1991 with &quot;Namibia, Land of the Brave&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Ishe Komborera Africa&quot;, a <b>Shona</b> translation of Enoch Sontonga's Xhosa hymn &quot;Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika&quot;, became Zimbabwe's first anthem, replaced in 1994 by &quot;Simudzai Mureza wedu WeZimbabwe&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rise-o-voices-of-rhodesia">Rise, O Voices of Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
